::: tip See it all live
**[Playground ↗](/playground)** is a second site, built inside this one, that uses every feature on this page: four markdown corpora, an OpenAPI reference rendered from JSON data, a dated blog, a version × locale switcher, and search. Each of them is a `content()` collection.
:::

[Remote functions](/docs/data-state/remote-functions) move data, but on most sites the largest body of data is the writing itself: docs, blog posts, changelogs, product pages, or entries in a CMS. You usually want two things from that writing. You want a navigation or an index that the client can fetch, and you want full rendered bodies on the page, ideally with interactive islands inside them. `@ogygia/content` gives SvelteKit content collections that do both and fit the islands model. This docs site is built on it, and every page you read here is a `.svx` entry rendered through it.

You define a collection once with `content({ loader, schema })`. The `loader` can point at a glob of markdown files, at a REST API or CMS, or at a source that pushes changes over time, and the way you read the collection (`refs`, `get`) stays the same whichever you choose.

What you get back depends on where you read it, because **the boundary picks the representation**. On the server you get the full entry, including its body, and across the wire you get plain data:

| read                     | returns                                               | where                |
| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- |
| `get(id)`                | `{ id, data, meta, body }`; the body is a held region | server only          |
| `list({ map })`          | a mapped data array                                   | over the wire        |
| `live.get` / `live.list` | mapped data, emitted again on each change             | over the wire (live) |

If your page is assembled from typed blocks rather than a single markdown body, use [Blocks](/docs/content/blocks), which is a separate, block-level API.

## Installing

Collections ship as their own package. Install it and list its plugin beside `ogygia()`:

```bash
pnpm add @ogygia/content
```

```ts title=vite.config.ts
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
import { content } from '@ogygia/content/vite';

plugins: [ogygia(), content({ markdown: {} }), sveltekit()]
```

The `markdown` option turns the markdown pipeline on; a collection over JSON, a CMS or an API needs only `content()`. On the dev server, the plugin reloads the page when a file under `src/content` changes; content kept elsewhere can be watched too, with the `watchDirs` option described in the [API reference](/docs/reference/api#ogygiavite-content). For markdown, `svelte.config.js` also takes `preprocess()` and `extensions()` from `@ogygia/content/vite` (see [Install](/docs/start/install)). To get the macro types (`import.meta.og.loader`, `og.code`, `og.md`), add one line to `src/ogygia.d.ts`:

```ts
/// <reference types="@ogygia/content/types" />
```

## Define the collection once, in a server module

A collection's `loader` holds the compiled corpus, so define the collection in a **`.server.ts`** file (or a `.remote.ts`, or under `src/lib/server/`). Kit then guarantees that no client module can import it, which means the corpus can never end up in a browser bundle:

```ts title=src/lib/collections.server.ts
import { content } from '@ogygia/content';
import * as v from 'valibot';

export const docs = content({
  // The loader is a compile macro — it owns the glob, you pass a literal path. Data + Meta are
  // inferred, no annotations. See the loader macro for markdown/folder/json/git.
  loader: import.meta.og.loader.markdown('../content/docs'),
  schema: v.object({ title: v.string(), section: v.string() }),
  filter: (e) => !e.data.draft   // visibility, declared ONCE
});
```

> `import.meta.og.loader.*` is the standard way to point a collection at static files. It takes care of the `import.meta.glob` plumbing, so you only write a literal path. It comes in four kinds (`markdown`, `folder`, `json` and `git`), all described on the **[loader macro](/docs/macros/loaders)** page along with its rules. The runtime builders it rewrites to (`markdown()`, `folder()`, `json()`) remain exported for the rare source you build by hand.

::: warning ogygia warns you
If you define a collection outside a server module, the build prints a warning that names the file. A client component that imported it would silently pull the whole corpus into its bundle, which is frequently megabytes. Keep the definition on the server and create the wire crossing in a `.remote.ts`; that split is the whole defence.
:::

The collection _handle_ is safe for the browser at the **type** level, because `docs.refs` and `docs.get` have no `$app/server` in their signatures. The _module that defines it_ is server-only, though, because of the glob. You can import the handle's types anywhere, but import the module only from the server.

The collection applies `filter` on every read path: `refs`, `get`, `live`, the wire remotes and prerender inputs. A draft is therefore invisible everywhere, including at its direct URL. A per-remote filter can narrow this further but never widen it.

### An entry is `{ id, data, meta, body }`

`get(id)` is server-only and returns the full entry:

- **`data`**: the validated frontmatter, typed from your `schema`.
- **`meta`**: output from the collectors, for example `meta.headings` for a table of contents.
- **`body`**: a [held region](/docs/regions/held-regions) that you render with `<Region>`.

`get` returns **`null`** when the id is unknown or filtered out, and it never throws, so the caller decides when to answer 404. Validate the slug in `load`:

```ts title=src/routes/docs/[...slug]/+page.server.ts
// clean 404 for a missing entry
import { error } from '@sveltejs/kit';
import { docs } from '$lib/collections.server';

export const load = async ({ params }) => {
  if (!(await docs.get(params.slug))) error(404, 'Not found');
};
```

Because the collection is server-only, the page component cannot import it. Instead it gets the entry from a `.remote.ts`, with a **baked body** that is allowed to cross the wire:

```ts title=src/lib/docs.remote.ts
// mint a getter that bakes the body into a region ticket
import { query } from '$app/server';
import * as v from 'valibot';
import { docs } from './collections.server';

export const entry = query(v.string(), async (slug) => {
  const e = await docs.get(slug);
  if (!e) return null;
  return { data: e.data, meta: e.meta, body: e.body ? await e.body : undefined };
});
```

```svelte title=src/routes/docs/[...slug]/+page.svelte
<!-- the body arrives as a ticket, islands inside wake -->
<script>
  import { page } from '$app/state';
  import { Region } from 'ogygia';
  import { entry } from '$lib/docs.remote';

  const view = (await entry(page.params.slug))!;
</script>

<h1>{view.data.title}</h1>
<Region of={view.body} />   <!-- a demo island in the .svx hydrates here -->
```

Awaiting `e.body` bakes its SSR HTML into the region ticket, and that is the only form of a body that can cross the wire; a raw body is a live render within the same pass and cannot be serialized. To prerender each entry, add this to `+page.server.ts`:

```ts title=src/routes/docs/[...slug]/+page.server.ts
export const prerender = true;
export const entries = async () => (await docs.refs()).map((e) => ({ slug: e.id }));
```

### Data for the wire: `list`

`list` and `live.list` create real Kit remotes, so create them in a `.remote.ts`, where Kit requires every export to be a remote. The consumer passes the arguments:

```ts title=src/lib/docs.remote.ts
import { withRemotes } from '@ogygia/content/server';
import { docs } from './collections.server';

export const docNav = withRemotes(docs).list({ map: (e) => ({ slug: e.id, title: e.data.title }) });
```

```svelte
<script>
  import { docNav } from '$lib/docs.remote';
  const nav = await docNav();
</script>
```

### Rendering an entry the client fetched

A body from `get()` is a live component, so it stays in the SSR pass and renders with `<Region of={entry.body} />` as shown above. When the client has to render an entry it fetched **over the wire** (an index, a live feed, a search result), map the entry to a held region of one of your own `with { region }` components and return that instead; `<Region>` fetches and hydrates it. [Held regions](/docs/regions/held-regions) explains how this works.

## Load markdown (`.svx` / `.md`)

`markdown()` is the built-in source for a glob of `.svx` / `.md` files. It runs an opinionated pipeline: mdsvex for the body, Shiki for code fences, pandoc-style heading ids, and a headings collector that fills each entry's `meta.headings`.

You turn the pipeline on with the `content()` plugin from `@ogygia/content/vite`, listed beside `ogygia()`. An empty `markdown: {}` uses the defaults: Shiki `github-light` / `github-dark` through `light-dark()`, pandoc `## Title {#id}` heading ids, collected `h2`–`h4`, and the wrapper class `code-only`.

```ts title=vite.config.ts
import { content } from '@ogygia/content/vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    ogygia(),
    content({
      markdown: {} // defaults: Shiki themes, heading ids, headings collector
    }),
    sveltekit()
  ]
});
```

The full option list (`themes`, `defaultColor`, `wrapperClass`, `headingIds`, `headings`, `langs`, `remarkPlugins`, `rehypePlugins`) is in the [API reference](/docs/reference/api). Every fenced code block on this site is highlighted by this pipeline.

### Headings without extra work

The preprocessor collects `h2`–`h4` into `meta.headings` and generates a slug for each heading that has no explicit `{#id}`. `get()` returns that array on the entry:

```svelte
<script>
  import { Region } from 'ogygia';
  const entry = await docs.get(slug);
</script>

<Toc headings={entry.meta.headings} />   <!-- [{ depth, id, text }] -->
<Region of={entry.body} />
```

The **On this page** rail beside these docs is built from that array. Build your own table of contents from `entry.meta.headings` rather than parsing the body again.

### Islands inside markdown

ogygia's transform runs after mdsvex, so marked island imports work inside a `.svx` file exactly as they do in a `.svelte` page:

```svelte
<script>
  import Counter from '$lib/Counter.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<Counter start={3} />
```

That is how the live demos in these docs run inside the prose.

## Custom sources (API / CMS)

A `loader` does not have to be a glob. Any object with `refs` / `get` is a source, so the same collection API covers a REST API or a CMS, and everything else (the schema, `filter`, the wire remotes) works exactly as it does for a file-backed collection.

| Source                    | Write                                                                                                       |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Static files in repo      | the [`loader` macro](/docs/macros/loaders): `import.meta.og.loader.markdown` · `.folder` · `.json` · `.git` |
| Blocks (CMS tree)         | [`blocks(source, registry)`](/docs/content/blocks) with a [`regions()`](/docs/macros/regions) registry      |
| A REST API                | a `{ refs, get }` source that fetches                                                                       |
| Something that **pushes** | add `live()` (a change signal) plus `live.list` / `live.get`                                                |

A source is two async methods: `refs()` lists the corpus as light metadata, and `get(id)` fetches one full entry.

```ts
import { content } from '@ogygia/content';

const press = content({
  schema,
  loader: {
    async refs() {
      const rows = await fetch('https://cms/posts').then((r) => r.json());
      return rows.map((r) => ({ id: r.slug, data: r }));
    },
    async get(id) {
      const row = await fetch(`https://cms/posts/${id}`).then((r) => r.json());
      return row ? { id, data: row } : null;
    }
  }
});
```

`get(id)` fetches a single post on demand rather than the whole list. Create the wire remotes in a `.remote.ts`, the same way as for a file-backed collection:

```ts
import { withRemotes } from '@ogygia/content/server';
export const list = withRemotes(press).list({ map: (e) => ({ id: e.id, title: e.data.title }) });
```

A source that fetches defaults to `query` mode, which means it is resolved at request time rather than at build time.

### `.yaml` / raw text (roll your own)

ogygia ships `markdown()`, `json()` and `blocks()`, but **not** a `.yaml` or raw-text loader, and its built-in YAML support covers frontmatter only. Either one takes a few lines over a glob with `defineSource`, which is also how the built-in sources are made, so you can bring whichever parser you prefer:

```ts
import { content, defineSource, toRawSource } from '@ogygia/content';
import { parse as parseYaml } from 'yaml'; // npm i yaml

// .yaml files → data-only entries
export const team = content({
  schema,
  loader: defineSource(
    toRawSource(import.meta.glob('./team/*.yaml', { query: '?raw', import: 'default', eager: true })),
    (raw) => ({ data: parseYaml(raw as string) })
  )
});

// raw text files → `{ body }`
export const snippets = content({
  loader: defineSource(
    toRawSource(import.meta.glob('./snippets/*.txt', { query: '?raw', import: 'default', eager: true })),
    (raw) => ({ data: { body: raw as string } })
  )
});
```

The second argument is a **format**, `(rawValue, id) => { data, meta?, body? }`, and that is the whole contract a source builder wraps.

## Live (push / SSE)

Add `live()`, a change **signal**: yield whenever your feed changes, and the collection reads the source again. Pair it with `live.list` / `live.get`, which emit fresh mapped snapshots on each change:

```ts
let posts = [];

const press = content({
  schema,
  loader: {
    async refs() { return posts.map((p) => ({ id: p.id, data: p })); },
    async get(id) { const p = posts.find((p) => p.id === id); return p ? { id: p.id, data: p } : null; },
    async *live() {
      for await (const next of subscribeToCms()) { posts = next; yield 1; }
    }
  }
});

export const headlines = withRemotes(press).live.list({ map: (e) => ({ id: e.id, title: e.data.title }) });
```

Yielding any value makes the collection read the whole source again. If you yield a **`string[]` of changed ids** instead, it reloads incrementally by calling `get(id)` for each, which is the better choice for a large collection where listing everything again would be wasteful.

### Watch it push

In the demo below, a `query.live` yields a rendered [held region](/docs/regions/held-regions) every second. The server renders each tick and pushes the HTML down the channel, and the client **morphs** it in place, with no client data code and no fetch per tick.

<script>
  import LivePartialDemo from '$lib/demos/LivePartialDemo.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<div class="doc-demo">
  <LivePartialDemo />
</div>

> Live sources need a long-lived server. On short-lived serverless functions an in-memory stream exists only within one isolate, so if you deploy there, say so in your own docs, degrade gracefully, and don't promise continuity.

## Do / don't

- **Do** define the collection once in a `.server.ts` module, reach it from server code (`get`), and create the wire crossings (`list` or a body-baking getter) in a `.remote.ts`.
- **Do** answer 404 in `load` with `error()`, not in the component's top-level `await`.
- **Do** start with `markdown: {}` at the defaults; the built-in Shiki themes and heading collector cover most sites.
- **Do** build your table of contents from `entry.meta.headings` instead of parsing the body again.
- **Do** map wire entries to a held region of your own `with { region }` component when the client must render them, because `<Region>` is the one renderable that crosses the wire.
- **Don't** register a separate mdsvex plugin; all configuration lives under `content({ markdown })`.
- **Don't** expect a compiled body from a CMS row. Those sources deliver **data**, which you render through a held region.
- **Don't** promise live continuity on serverless, where an in-memory stream exists only within one isolate.

---

_A collection knows what exists, and [`site()`](/docs/content/site) decides how it reads: navigation, search, the shell and more._
